      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the number of four-digit odd numbers (\\(A\\)), we need to count all the four-digit numbers where the units digit is odd. The four-digit number starts with 1000.\n\n1. Identify the range of four-digit numbers: \n   \\[\n   1000 \\leq n \\leq 9999\n   \\]\n\n2. Determine the smallest four-digit odd number:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Smallest four-digit number} = 1001\n   \\]\n   The units digit must be odd: \\(1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\).\n   Thus, the smallest odd number is:\n   \\[\n   1001\n   \\]\n\n3. Determine the largest four-digit odd number:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Largest four-digit number} = 9999\n   \\]\n   The units digit must be odd: \\(1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\).\n   Thus, the largest odd number is:\n   \\[\n   9999\n   \\]\n\n4. Count the possible units digits:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Possible units digits} = 1, 3, 5, 7, 9\n   \\]\n\n5. Calculate the number of valid four-digit odd numbers:\n   There are 5 choices for the units digit.\n   The remaining three digits can be any digit from 0 to 9:\n   \\[\n   10 \\times 10 \\times 10 = 1000\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the number of four-digit odd numbers is:\n   \\[\n   A = 1000\n   \\]\n\nTo find the number of four-digit multiples of 5 (\\(B\\)), we need to count all the four-digit numbers divisible by 5.\n\n1. Identify the range of four-digit numbers:\n   \\[\n   1000 \\leq n \\leq 9999\n   \\]\n\n2. Determine the smallest four-digit number that is a multiple of 5:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Smallest four-digit number} = 1000\n   \\]\n   The units digit must be 0 or 5:\n   Thus, the smallest multiple of 5 is:\n   \\[\n   1000\n   \\]\n\n3. Determine the largest four-digit number that is a multiple of 5:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Largest four-digit number} = 9995\n   \\]\n   The units digit must be 0 or 5:\n   Thus, the largest multiple of 5 is:\n   \\[\n   9995\n   \\]\n\n4. Count the possible units digits:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Possible units digits} = 0, 5\n   \\]\n\n5. Calculate the number of valid four-digit multiples of 5:\n   There are 2 choices for the units digit.\n   The remaining three digits can be any digit from 0 to 9:\n   \\[\n   10 \\times 10 \\times 10 = 1000\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the number of four-digit multiples of 5 is:\n   \\[\n   B = 1000\n   \\]\n\nFinally, add the two results to find \\(A + B\\):\n\\[\nA + B = 1000 + 1000 = 2000\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2000}\n\\]",
